12:23:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really sharp observation—and you’re right, the way FOIA successes are reported can make it look like you just file a request and get the whole file. The reality is more complicated, and reporters deal with redactions and denials all the time. What you see in a news story is usually the *result* of a lot of work, not a simple handover. Here’s why those stories might give the impression that FOIA is a “guaranteed win”: 1. **Selection bias in the news.** Reporters file a huge number of FOIA requests. Many come back heavily redacted, stonewalled, or tied up in years of litigation. Those don’t make headlines. The ones that become stories are the rare, newsworthy disclosures—often documents that were *mostly* released because no exemption applied, or because the agency chose not to fight. When you read “obtained via FOIA,” it sounds effortless, but behind that line could be months of negotiation, appeals, and sometimes lawsuits that forced the agency to release more than it initially wanted. 2. **Not all information is exempt.** A lot of government records simply don’t fall into any exemption bucket. Routine emails, final reports, statistical data, policy statements, contracts, and many other documents are totally releasable. An agency might hand them over without a single black bar because there’s no legal basis to withhold anything. When a journalist gets a clean set of documents, it’s often because the request targeted *exactly* the kind of information FOIA was designed to expose. That doesn’t mean redactions don’t exist; it just means that particular request hit the “presumption of access” sweet spot. 3. **Reporters often ask for specific, non-exempt things.** Experienced requesters know how to frame requests to avoid triggering exemptions—asking for final decisions rather than drafts, aggregate data rather than individual personal info, or records that are by law already public. So they increase the chances of a clean release. 4. **“We got the documents” doesn’t mean “they gave us everything.”** Even when a story says “documents obtained through FOIA,” the released files may still have redactions—just not on the parts the reporter is writing about. The lede might be based on unredacted portions, while entire pages are blacked out. The reporter might not mention the redactions because they’re irrelevant to the story. In fact, sometimes redacted pages are a story unto themselves (showing what the government is hiding), but if the unredacted material is already damning enough, the reporter may simply move forward. 5. **The law applies pressure, which often leads to voluntary releases.** Knowing that a requester can appeal and go to court, agencies often err on the side of release to avoid lawsuits. They might withhold the truly exempt bits and release the rest, or perhaps even release borderline material if they think a judge would force them anyway. So the “threat” of FOIA litigation, combined with the requirement to justify every redaction, can produce a relatively clean document. So while the Freedom of Information Act absolutely permits redactions and refusals within its exemptions, it also creates a strong system of accountability that, in practice, often delivers substantial information. Those news stories aren’t evidence that FOIA is a loophole-free guarantee; they’re evidence that when the law works as intended, the public gets access to a lot that the government would rather keep quiet—even if some parts stay blacked out. The name doesn’t mean “every piece of information,” but it does mean that, by default, the government must open its files unless it can point to a specific, legally recognized harm. That default is powerful, and it’s why so many requests succeed.
