12:25:19 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're absolutely right that the word “shall” in FOIA is mandatory language. In fact, Section 552(a) says, “Each agency shall make available to the public information as follows...” That “shall” creates a legal duty, not a suggestion. But here’s the critical nuance: **the duty is defined by the rest of the sentence and the statute as a whole.** A “shall” duty can be—and routinely is—subject to conditions and exceptions that are written into the same law. For example, a statute might say, “Every employer shall pay overtime, except for employees classified as exempt under section 213.” The “shall” makes it mandatory, but the scope of that mandatory obligation is explicitly limited by the exemptions. You wouldn’t say the overtime requirement is “full of loopholes” because certain workers are carved out; you’d say the law defines precisely to whom the duty applies. FOIA works exactly the same way. The “shall make available” language in subsection (a) requires agencies to disclose records upon request, **except** those records—or portions of records—that are covered by the nine specific exemptions in subsection (b). Subsection (b) begins: “This section does not apply to matters that are—(1) specifically authorized under criteria established by an Executive order to be kept secret in the interest of national defense or foreign policy...” and so on through the nine exemptions. So the mandatory “shall” doesn’t vanish; it just only applies to the universe of records that aren’t excluded by the exemptions. The exemptions aren’t loopholes—they’re part of the same statutory section that tells you what the “shall” covers. An agency that redacts properly is fulfilling its mandatory duty under the Act, because the Act itself mandates that those specific categories are *not* subject to mandatory disclosure. In other words, FOIA says: “For records that fall within the exemptions, you ‘shall not’ disclose them—or at least you are permitted not to.” (Some e