12:31:17 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a genuinely insightful question. If disclosure were truly the automatic outcome, you’d expect agencies to just hit “send” with no review. But the fact that they maintain large FOIA offices and legal staff actually **confirms** the default, rather than undermining it. Let me explain why. ### 1. The default requires careful segregation The law’s default isn’t “hand over everything blindly.” It’s: “Disclose all records **except** those portions that are exempt, and you must provide the exempt portions’ legal justification.” So the agency *cannot* just dump raw files on requesters. It has a mandatory duty to: - Locate all responsive records. - Go through them page by page, line by line. - Identify any exempt material. - Segregate and release the non-exempt parts. - Cite the specific exemption for each redaction. This is a labor-intensive legal obligation. The FOIA office isn’t a barrier to disclosure—it’s the engine that carries it out. Without that staff, the agency couldn’t fulfill its duty at all. The volume is staggering: the Department of Homeland Security alone received over 60,000 requests in a recent year. No agency head can personally review records; a dedicated office is the only way to make the default functional. ### 2. Much of what the government holds is inherently mixed with sensitive material A single FBI investigative file might contain agent analysis (deliberative), names of subjects (privacy), informant identities (law enforcement), and classified intelligence (national security). The agency can’t just withhold the whole file—the default forces it to review every page to redact only what’s exempt and release the rest. That’s why you sometimes see pages with just a few names blacked out: the default worked, but the sensitive nuggets needed to be removed. The more sensitive the records, the more review is required. That’s not a secretive impulse; it’s the legally mandated process that produces the maximum lawful disclosure. ### 3. Lawyers are needed because the exemptions require legal interpretation You earlier objected to the deliberative process exemption as too vague. That vagueness is exactly *why* lawyers are involved: to argue about its edges, to build a defensible record for the requester and any future court challenge, and to keep the agency within the law. If the agency just guessed, it would lose in court more often and might release things it shouldn’t, or withhold things it shouldn’t. Legal review is part of the accountability system. ### 4. The federal scale is incomparable to city government You compared to your city’s records law. A city might process a few hundred requests a year, mostly for routine documents with minimal protected information. The federal government processes nearly a million requests a year, for records that include classified intelligence, trade secrets, medical data, and diplomatic cables. The difference in volume and sensitivity directly explains the difference in infrastructure. It’s not that the federal government is less committed to openness; it’s that the scale and content demand a formal process. Even your city almost certainly has a records officer, and they probably consult the city attorney’s office when a request raises privacy or law enforcement issues. ### 5. The “name suggests automatic handover” rhetoric doesn’t match how any complex law works The name is a principle; the text is the mechanism. The Civil Rights Act didn’t end discrimination just by being named that. It created enforcement agencies and legal standards that require lawyers and offices to implement. FOIA is no different. The presence of a FOIA office is the *result* of the disclosure default, not evidence of a culture of withholding. In short: the FOIA office exists **because** disclosure is the default, and the law forces that office to do the painstaking work of opening files while protecting what’s rightfully exempt. It’s the grunt work of the default, not the subversion of it.
