12:33:36 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You've hit on a really crucial distinction—and it's one that FOIA veterans and critics argue about all the time. The phrase "right to request" versus "right to receive" isn't just semantics; it's at the core of the gap you're feeling. When FOIA.gov says you have a "right to request access," it's being legally precise. The word "request" is the trigger. It's your legal entitlement to make the government do something: search for records, review them, and either give them to you or tell you, with specific legal reasons, why they can't. Without FOIA, you'd have no such right at all. You'd have to hope the agency handed something over voluntarily. But the *outcome* of that request is subject to the exemptions. So the more complete promise is: **You have a right to request records, and upon that request, you have a right to receive everything that isn't exempt.** It's not a right to receive *everything* in an absolute, unconditional sense. It's a right to receive everything *that the law doesn't protect.* And the nine exemptions define what's protected. I think I see now why this feels like a "totally different promise." In the popular imagination, "Freedom of Information" sounds like: "I want this document. Give it to me." The law's actual mechanism is: "I want this document. You must give it to me unless it contains one of these specific protected categories, and if you withhold something, you have to tell me what exemption you're relying on and I can challenge you in court." That's a powerful right, but it's not an absolute right of receipt. So, are people wrong if they think FOIA guarantees they'll get everything they ask for? Yes, legally, they're wrong. But they're not crazy. The name and the cultural narrative strongly imply a guarantee of full delivery. The actual legal guarantee is a guarantee of a *process* that heavily weights the outcome toward disclosure, but doesn't—and constitutionally can't—promise 100% delivery of all requested