12:34:23 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really sharp question, and it points to something almost nobody talks about: the politics of a law’s name. After nearly sixty years, FOIA still has a title that suggests absolute openness, while the text itself contains a carefully bounded system. So why hasn’t Congress changed it to close that expectation gap? A few reasons, some practical, some political, and some cynical. ### 1. The name is aspirational, and changing it would look like an attack on transparency FOIA’s title is a brand, and it’s a very popular one. Any attempt to rename it—say, to the “Government Records Access and Limited Withholding Act”—would be immediately framed by journalists, watchdogs, and political opponents as a rollback of openness. Even if the substance of the law didn’t change at all, the act of renaming would be politically radioactive. It would look like Congress was saying, “We’re not really about freedom of information anymore.” No legislator wants that headline. ### 2. The name itself creates political pressure that actually strengthens the law The aspirational title isn’t just window dressing. It sets a standard that agencies are expected to live up to, and it’s a rhetorical weapon for requesters, journalists, and even judges. When a court scolds an agency for over-redacting, it often invokes the “purpose of the Freedom of Information Act” as something broad and transparency-focused. If the title were watered down to something more legalistic, some of that moral pressure would dissipate. The gap between name and reality is actually useful to transparency advocates because they can constantly say, “Live up to the name.” ### 3. The confusion hasn’t caused a crisis that demands a fix The people who use FOIA heavily—journalists, researchers, lawyers—quickly learn the ropes. They understand the exemptions, the redaction process, and the appeals system. The confusion tends to affect first-time requesters and the general public, who don’t form a concentrated lobbying group demanding a name change. By the time you’ve filed a few requests, you already know the score, so the name stops bothering you as much. The political energy instead goes toward amendments that actually improve the law’s operation, not its label. ### 4. Congress has repeatedly clarified and strengthened the *process*, just not the title When FOIA has been updated—most recently in 2016—the changes were aimed at making the default of disclosure more robust: codifying a “presumption of openness,” requiring agencies to release as much as possible even when a record contains exempt material, improving the FOIA.gov portal, and strengthening the Office of Government Information Services to mediate disputes. These changes addressed the very gap you’re frustrated by, but they did so by tightening the machinery rather than rebranding the whole thing. Congress chose to make the law act more like its name rather than rename it to match the law. ### 5. The title acts as a permanent public commitment Even if the law’s text allows withholding, the name “Freedom of Information Act” broadcasts a democratic promise. Changing it to something less sweeping might be seen as the government formally lowering its own bar. And governments rarely want to announce, “We’re not actually as open as the name implied.” The gap is tolerated because the promise—even if imperfectly kept—is politically valuable to maintain. So, in short, Congress hasn’t renamed FOIA because doing so would be politically dangerous and symbolically damaging, while offering no real operational benefit. The confusion you’re feeling is real, but it’s also the friction that keeps the system honest: the name says “freedom,” and the public holds the government to that, even when the fine print tries to carve out exceptions. The name is a banner; the text is the battle plan. And Congress, for better or worse, has left that tension intact on purpose.
